  {
    "node_index": 164,
    "tag": "h1",
    "text": "How many chromosomes do people have?",
    "label": "unreviewed",
    "formatting": {}
  },
  {
    "node_index": 171,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "In humans, each cell normally contains 23 pairs of chromosomes, for a total of 46. Twenty-two of these pairs, called autosomes, look the same in both males and females. The 23rd pair, the sex chromosomes, differ between males and females. Females have two copies of the X chromosome, while males have one X and one Y chromosome.",
    "label": "unreviewed",
    "formatting": {}
  },
  {
    "node_index": 176,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The 22 autosomes are numbered by size. The other two chromosomes, X and Y, are the sex chromosomes. This picture of the human chromosomes lined up in pairs is called a karyotype.",
    "label": "unreviewed",
    "formatting": {}
  },
  {
    "node_index": 187,
    "tag": "h2",
    "text": "For more information about the 23 pairs of human chromosomes:",
    "label": "unreviewed",
    "formatting": {}
  },
  {
    "node_index": 189,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "MedlinePlus Genetics provides information about each human chromosome written in lay language.",
    "label": "unreviewed",
    "formatting": {}
  },
  {
    "node_index": 193,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "MedlinePlus offers general information about chromosomes and karyotype tests.",
    "label": "unreviewed",
    "formatting": {}
  },
  {
    "node_index": 198,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The University of Utah's Genetic Science Learning Center discusses how karyotypes can be used in diagnosing genetic disorders.",
    "label": "unreviewed",
    "formatting": {}
  },
